An angle inscribed in a semicircle is a right angle, so $\angle ABC=90^\circ$, and $AC=2\cdot13=26$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0),A=(-13,0),C=(13,0);
pair B=A+10*dir(degrees(acos(10/26)));
draw(circle(O,13),linewidth(0.8));
draw(A--C,linewidth(0.8));draw(A--B--C,linewidth(0.9));
pair u=unit(A-B),v=unit(C-B);
draw((B+1.3*u)--(B+1.3*u+1.3*v)--(B+1.3*v),linewidth(0.4));
dot(O);
label("$A$",A,W);label("$C$",C,E);label("$B$",B,NW);label("$P$",O,S);
label("$10$",(A+B)/2,NW);label("$26$",(A+C)/2+(4,0),S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

By the Pythagorean theorem, $BC=\sqrt{26^2-10^2}=\sqrt{576}=\boxed{24}$ cm.

The displaced volume is the volume of the part of the cylinder below the water surface: the length $16$ times the area of the circular segment of height $2$ in a circle of radius $4$ (the cross-section).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=4,h=2;
pair O=(0,0);
real yw=-r+h; real xw=sqrt(r^2-(r-h)^2);
real t=degrees(acos((r-h)/r));
path seg=arc(O,r,270-t,270+t)--cycle;
fill(seg,rgb(0.75,0.85,1));
draw(circle(O,r),linewidth(0.9));
draw((-6,yw)--(6,yw),blue+linewidth(0.6));
draw(O--(xw,yw),dashed);draw(O--(-xw,yw),dashed);draw(O--(0,yw),dashed);
dot(O);label("$O$",O,N);
label("$4$",(xw/2,yw/2),SE);label("$2$",(0,yw),NW);
draw(arc(O,0.9,270-t,270+t),linewidth(0.5));label("$120^\circ$",O+1.3*dir(270)+(0.0,0.0),E,fontsize(8pt));
label("water level",(6,yw),N,blue);
draw((-0.25,-r)--(0.25,-r));draw((-0.25,yw)--(0.25,yw));
label("$h=2$",(0,-r),S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The water line is at distance $4-2=2$ from the centre $O$, so its half-chord is $\sqrt{16-4}=2\sqrt3$ and it subtends an angle $2\cos^{-1}\dfrac{2}{4}=120^\circ$ at $O$. The segment's area is the sector minus the triangle:
\[
\dfrac{120}{360}\pi\cdot4^2-\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot4\sqrt3\cdot2=\dfrac{16\pi}{3}-4\sqrt3 .
\]
The displaced volume is
\[
16\left(\dfrac{16\pi}{3}-4\sqrt3\right)=\boxed{\dfrac{256\pi}{3}-64\sqrt3}\approx157.2\text{ cubic inches}.
\]

$BC$ is the hypotenuse and $AB$ is opposite $C$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(0,3),C=(5,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw((0.35,0)--(0.35,0.35)--(0,0.35),linewidth(0.4));
draw(arc(C,0.8,180-degrees(atan(3/5)),180),linewidth(0.6));label("$C$",C,SE);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,NW);
label("$3$",(A+B)/2,W);label("$x$",(B+C)/2,NE);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

So $\sin C=\dfrac{AB}{BC}=\dfrac{3}{x}=k$, giving $x=\dfrac{3}{k}$. By the Pythagorean theorem,
\[
AC=\sqrt{x^2-9}=\sqrt{\dfrac{9}{k^2}-9}=\boxed{\dfrac{3\sqrt{1-k^2}}{k}}\qquad(0<k<1).
\]
(Equivalently $AC=\dfrac{AB}{\tan C}=3\cdot\dfrac{\cos C}{\sin C}$.)

Place the centre at the origin with $A=(4,0)$; then $C=(-2,2\sqrt3)$ and $D=(-4,0)$, so $K=(-3,\sqrt3)$. Let $H=(-3,0)$ be the foot of the perpendicular from $K$ to the long diagonal $AD$.

\begin{asy}
